AI-Based Drowsiness Detection System
Part 2 (Questions 21–40)
Q21. Explain your code flow line by line.
Answer

When the program starts, it first imports all the required libraries:

OpenCV for webcam access and displaying frames.
NumPy for mathematical operations.
time to measure how long the eyes remain closed.  
pygame to play the alarm sound.
MediaPipe for face detection and facial landmark prediction.

After importing, I initialize the MediaPipe Face Landmarker using the .task model file.

Next, I initialize the Pygame mixer so the audio engine is ready before any detection occurs.

Then I open the webcam using cv2.VideoCapture(0).

Inside the infinite loop:

Capture one frame.
Convert BGR → RGB.
Pass the RGB frame to MediaPipe.
Get facial landmarks.
Extract eye landmarks.
Calculate whether the eyes are open or closed.
Update the timer.
Play or stop the alarm.
Draw landmarks and status text.
Display the frame.

The loop repeats until the user presses Q.

Follow-up

Why do we initialise everything before entering the loop?

Because model loading, audio initialisation, and webcam setup are expensive operations. Initialising them once avoids unnecessary overhead and keeps the application fast.

Q22. Why do you use an infinite loop?
Answer

A webcam continuously generates frames.

Unlike image processing, where only one image is analysed, a real-time application must process frames continuously.

The infinite loop allows the application to:

Read a frame
Process it
Display it
Repeat

Without this loop, the application would process only a single frame and then terminate.

Follow-up

Won't an infinite loop consume all CPU resources?

It can if not handled carefully.

However:

Camera FPS naturally limits execution.
MediaPipe processing takes time.
The loop exits when the user presses Q.

So CPU usage remains manageable.

Q23. Why do you check the return value (ret) after reading a frame?
Answer

ret indicates whether the webcam successfully captured a frame.

If ret is False, it means:

Camera disconnected
Camera busy
Hardware failure
Frame capture failed

Processing an invalid frame would lead to exceptions or crashes.

Therefore I immediately stop processing if ret == False.

This is good defensive programming.

Follow-up

What happens if you ignore ret?

Functions like cv2.cvtColor() will receive None instead of an image and raise an exception.

Q24. Why do you convert the frame to a MediaPipe Image object?
Answer

MediaPipe Tasks API does not directly accept a NumPy array.

It expects a mediapipe.Image.

This object stores:

image format
width
height
pixel data

Converting to this format ensures compatibility with the MediaPipe inference pipeline.

Follow-up

Why didn't older MediaPipe versions require this?

Older APIs directly accepted NumPy arrays.

The newer Tasks API introduces a standardised image abstraction for better consistency across different platforms.

Q25. Why don't you create the FaceLandmarker inside the loop?
Answer

Creating the model is computationally expensive.

If I initialise it inside the loop:

Model loads every frame.
Memory allocations happen repeatedly.
FPS drops dramatically.
CPU usage increases.

Therefore I initialise the model only once before entering the processing loop.

Interviewer says:

Suppose you initialise it every frame.

What happens?

The application becomes extremely slow because model loading is much slower than inference.

Q26. What exactly is happening during inference?
Answer

Inference means running a trained neural network on new input data.

For every frame:

The RGB image is fed into the trained model.
The model performs forward propagation.
Features are extracted.
The network predicts landmark coordinates.
The predicted landmarks are returned.

No learning happens during inference.

The model parameters remain unchanged.

Follow-up

Difference between training and inference?

Training updates weights.

Inference only uses learned weights.

Q27. Why is inference slower than normal OpenCV operations?
Answer

OpenCV operations such as drawing circles or converting colour spaces are algorithmic image-processing operations.

MediaPipe inference runs a deep neural network.

Neural networks perform:

Matrix multiplication
Convolution
Activation functions

These computations are much heavier than simple OpenCV functions.

Therefore inference consumes more CPU time.

Q28. How many landmarks does MediaPipe return?
Answer

The Face Landmarker predicts 478 landmarks in the latest models.

These include:

Eyes
Eyebrows
Nose
Lips
Chin
Face contour
Iris landmarks

Each landmark has:

x
y
z

coordinates.

Follow-up

Did you use all 478?

No.

I extracted only the eye landmarks because my application only needs information related to eye closure.

Q29. Why don't you simply crop the eye image and classify it using CNN?
Answer

That is another valid approach.

However it requires:

Dataset
Manual labelling
Model training
Model evaluation
Deployment

MediaPipe already provides highly accurate eye landmarks.

Therefore I don't need to build another CNN from scratch.

This reduces complexity while maintaining excellent performance.

Q30. Explain how your eye detection logic works.
Answer

MediaPipe provides predefined landmark indices corresponding to both eyes.

Using those indices:

I retrieve the eye landmark coordinates.
I analyse the relative positions of the eyelids.
If the upper and lower eyelids come very close together, the eye is considered closed.
If the distance remains sufficiently large, the eye is considered open.

Instead of relying on pixel colours, the decision is based on facial geometry, making it more robust to different skin tones and lighting conditions.

Follow-up

Why not detect eye colour?

Because eye colour has no relationship with whether the eyes are open or closed.

Q31. Why do you measure time instead of counting frames?
Answer

Frame count depends on FPS.

Example:

30 frames

means:

1 second at 30 FPS
0.5 seconds at 60 FPS
2 seconds at 15 FPS

This makes frame counting unreliable.

Time measurement is independent of FPS.

Therefore it provides consistent behaviour across different computers.

Interviewer Follow-up

Which approach is better?

Time-based detection.

Q32. Why did you use Python's time module?
Answer

The time module provides accurate timestamps.

Whenever the eyes first close, I record the current time.

As long as the eyes remain closed, I calculate:

Current Time − Start Time

If the elapsed duration exceeds my threshold, I classify the user as drowsy.

When the eyes open again, I reset the timer.

This makes the detection stable and independent of processing speed.

Q33. Why do you reset the timer after opening the eyes?
Answer

Suppose someone closes their eyes for one second.

Later they blink normally.

If I don't reset the timer, the old value continues accumulating.

Eventually, even a normal blink could trigger the alarm.

Resetting ensures each closed-eye event is evaluated independently.

Q34. Why did you choose Pygame for audio?
Answer

Pygame provides a lightweight audio engine that is easy to integrate into Python applications.

Advantages include:

Fast initialisation
Non-blocking playback
Simple API
Supports common audio formats
Minimal dependencies

For a real-time application, it allows the alarm to play without interrupting the video processing loop.

Follow-up

Why not use playsound()?

Because playsound() is blocking in many implementations.

It pauses program execution until playback finishes.

That would freeze the webcam stream.

Pygame plays audio asynchronously.

Q35. Why don't you call play() every frame?
Answer

Suppose the application runs at 30 FPS.

Calling play() every frame means 30 play requests per second.

This causes:

Audio overlap
Distorted sound
Increased CPU usage
Repeated restarting of the alarm

Instead, I first check whether the alarm is already playing.

Only if it is not playing do I start playback.

Interviewer Follow-up

How do you know if it's already playing?

Using Pygame's audio channel state or maintaining a Boolean flag indicating whether the alarm has already been triggered.

Q36. Why stop the alarm immediately when the eyes open?
Answer

The objective of the alarm is to alert the user while they are drowsy.

Once the eyes reopen, the alert is no longer necessary.

Stopping the alarm immediately prevents unnecessary noise and improves the user experience.

It also prepares the system for future drowsiness events.

Q37. How would you calculate FPS?
Answer

FPS stands for Frames Per Second.

It represents how many frames the application processes every second.

A common method is:

FPS = 1 / (Current Time − Previous Frame Time)

A higher FPS results in smoother and more responsive real-time detection.

Follow-up

Is higher FPS always better?

No.

Very high FPS increases CPU usage.

The goal is to maintain stable real-time performance with acceptable resource consumption.

Q38. Why doesn't your application freeze while the alarm is playing?
Answer

Because Pygame handles audio playback independently after it has been started.

Once playback begins, the main thread continues processing webcam frames.

This allows video capture, landmark detection, and screen updates to continue smoothly while the alarm is playing in the background.

Q39. What are the biggest performance bottlenecks in your project?
Answer

The most computationally expensive components are:

Neural network inference in MediaPipe.
Continuous webcam frame capture.
Rendering facial landmarks on every frame.

In comparison, operations such as colour conversion, timer calculations, and text rendering consume very little processing time.

Optimising inference provides the greatest performance improvement.

Q40. If your FPS suddenly drops from 30 to 10, how would you debug it?
Answer

I would investigate the problem systematically:

Measure FPS to confirm the issue.
Check CPU and memory usage.
Verify whether MediaPipe inference time has increased.
Ensure the webcam resolution is not unnecessarily high.
Check if the alarm playback or other background processes are consuming resources.
Profile the application to identify slow functions.
Reduce frame resolution or optimise rendering if necessary.

Rather than guessing, I would profile each stage of the pipeline to identify the actual bottleneck and optimise only that component.
